{"id":3309,"date":"2021-07-26T11:59:45","date_gmt":"2021-07-26T18:59:45","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3309"},"modified":"2024-09-01T13:47:59","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T20:47:59","slug":"advanced-ir-functions","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/advanced-ir-functions\/","title":{"rendered":"2.3: Advanced IR Functions"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150264c83\" name=\"6a22150264c83\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Introduction to obstacle avoidance<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Have you ever been in a car when the emergency auto brakes went on? What happened? How was the car able to tell when to stop? Self-driving cars need to be able to avoid obstacles in an emergency. Instead of infrared, they use more advanced sensors like <\/span><b>LIDAR<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> (Light Imaging Detection and Ranging) and <\/span><b>RADAR<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. LIDAR uses laser light to map out the environment while RADAR uses radio waves. Despite not having this technology, Zumi can still avoid obstacles!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2064&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150264d95\" name=\"6a22150264d95\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Stop for pedestrians<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It is extremely important for self-driving cars to be able to detect dangers up ahead, especially if they are pedestrians! In the code below, Zumi drives forward using forward_step() until she detects your hand (or another object) with either the front left or front right IR sensor. When you take your hand away, Zumi should keep driving. At the end of the loop, don\u2019t forget to add a stop in case Zumi doesn\u2019t detect any obstacles.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3311&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Now have Zumi drive in reverse and check her back IR sensors![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150264de8\" name=\"6a22150264de8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Avoid collision functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]That&#8217;s a lot of code to write just to avoid some obstacles! So we made a function for you that you can use to drive until an obstacle is detected. Once an obstacle has been detected, the function will stop running, even if the duration has not been completed. If there was no obstacle detected during the timeout, Zumi will stop automatically. Try it out![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3313&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Try going in reverse at an angle while checking the back sensors.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3314&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150264e38\" name=\"6a22150264e38\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge: Escape a Maze<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Collision avoidance is an essential feature in any car, but a self-driving car would have to go around the obstacles in addition to stopping for them. In this section, you will learn how to change direction if Zumi encounters an obstacle and write a program to have Zumi escape a maze!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2065&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For this activity, you will need both of your front IR sensors. This time, instead of stopping and waiting for the obstacle to be removed, you will tell Zumi to turn a little to the left or to the right to go around, depending on which sensor is triggered.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150264e90\" name=\"6a22150264e90\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Initialize heading<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Start by creating a variable &#8220;heading&#8221; that is initialized to 0. Then, set up a loop with\u00a0<strong>two<\/strong> if statements. Why two?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the right sensor detects an obstacle, then Zumi&#8217;s angle should change a few degrees to the left<\/li>\n<li>If the left sensor detects an obstacle, then Zumi&#8217;s angle should change a few degrees to the right<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>There will be no else statement because Zumi will continue driving regardless if the object is on the left or on the right.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3317&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150264ed4\" name=\"6a22150264ed4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Check sensors<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Since Zumi will do something different depending on which IR sensor is detected, separate the right sensor and left sensor detection into their own if statements.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3316&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150264f26\" name=\"6a22150264f26\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Change heading<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]If an object is on Zumi&#8217;s right side, then Zumi will need to turn to left. Remember that in mathematics, turning to the left means the degrees\u00a0<strong>increase<\/strong> while turning to the right degrees\u00a0<strong>decrease.\u00a0<\/strong>Add or subtract 30 degrees in the if statements.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3318&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]The last step in the loop before it repeats is to drive in that direction. You will be using the forward_step() block. Notice that since the angle is always changing, we use the heading variable instead which is keeping track of which direction Zumi should be going in.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3319&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150264f72\" name=\"6a22150264f72\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Line following<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You&#8217;ve learned how the front and back IR sensors can help Zumi avoid obstacles, but what about line following or staying with in the lanes on the road? You&#8217;re in luck! We&#8217;ve built a line follow function for you![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2071&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Since this is all about bottom IR sensors, this function uses both of them to check the line. Pretend you are walking on a piece of tape on the floor. If your right foot steps off the line, which way do you turn? What about your left foot? The IR sensors use the same logic.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2072&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150264fd0\" name=\"6a22150264fd0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>line_follow_gyro()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Let\u2019s test the function! Use electrical tape or the driving school mat (if you have one) and line Zumi up on the starting black line. You can download a line following worksheet <a href=\"https:\/\/drive.google.com\/file\/d\/1QQRkhAL7k7et0Qm_h0UMTNAopj15zqfI\/view\">here.<\/a> The function line_follow_gyro() needs many parameters. Set the speed to a relatively low speed and set the program for 3 seconds (or more, depending on how long your line is!).<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3320&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you think that Zumi is not detecting the lines very well, or that the difference between dark and light is not big enough, you might need to adjust the <\/span><b>thresholds<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, or limits, that the IR sensors will detect. Their default is set to 100. Refer to the IR Sensor lesson to test your IR sensors and figure out your thresholds.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>If you think that Zumi is turning too little or too much, change the\u00a0<strong>angle adjustment\u00a0<\/strong>parameter.<\/p>\n<p><b>Note<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">: This function calls a while loop. If both of the IR sensors see white (the end of the line), the code will automatically stop, even if the time was not completed.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150265027\" name=\"6a22150265027\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Review<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this lesson, you learned how to use Zumi\u2019s IR sensors to avoid collisions with objects. You also learned how to combine that collision avoidance with Zumi\u2019s heading to make Zumi move around those obstacles. Finally, you learned how to use Zumi\u2019s bottom IR sensors to have Zumi follow lines.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2938&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 11 Introduction to obstacle avoidance [vc_column_text]Have you ever been in a car when the emergency auto brakes went on? What happened? How was the car able to tell&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/advanced-ir-functions\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2897,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":2878,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2195,2108,2240,2243,2241,2122,2126,2230],"class_list":["post-3309","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-advanced","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-ir","lesson-tag-line-following","lesson-tag-obstacle-avoidance","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-senior","lesson-tag-zumi","module-senior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3309","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":6,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3309\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":12832,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3309\/revisions\/12832"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/2897"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=3309"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=3309"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=3309"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}